# apps/ itself ships nothing. Only its src/<component>/fleet.yaml directories are
# deployable bundles — that is the contract bin/fleet-apply implements, and Rancher's
# GitRepo (paths: [apps]) must agree.
#
# Without this file Rancher builds an extra CATCH-ALL bundle for the `apps` path itself,
# holding every file under apps/ that no nested fleet.yaml already claims: PNG/JPEG
# assets (apps/blueorange/src/assets, apps/docs/images), lockfiles, Astro/React sources,
# pyinfra code — 10.4 MiB across 642 files, none of it Kubernetes. The Bundle then
# exceeds the kube-apiserver's 3 MiB max request body and the GitRepo fails with
#   Request entity too large: limit is 3145728
# Nothing catches this locally, because bin/fleet-apply only ever applies the nested
# fleet.yaml directories and never builds the catch-all. See bin/fleet-build.
#
# Ignoring everything leaves the catch-all with no resources, so Fleet skips creating it
# and only the 27 real component bundles remain. Nested fleet.yaml bundles read their own
# directory and are unaffected, as are the `file://` chart dependencies umbrella bundles
# resolve against apps/platform/src/netbird-reverse-proxy-shared (that resolution reads
# the filesystem, not the bundle).
*
